# A WORKFLOW IS A GRAPH, AND A GRAPH IS NOT LINE-MERGEABLE.
#
# Measured 2026-10-31 on the South London example, two authors, two clones. Without this
# line, git merged two authors' edits to DIFFERENT NODES of the same workflow silently:
# the result was a graph neither of them drew, and nobody reviewed the combination.
# File granularity alone does not prevent that, because git merges by hunk and not by file.
#
# With it, the three cases come out as intended:
#   different workflows ................ clean merge
#   same workflow, different nodes ..... conflict
#   same workflow, same node ........... conflict
#
# It also makes a checkout behave the way the GitHub Contents API already does from the
# browser, where a blob-sha precondition refuses a stale write rather than interleaving it.
# Two co-owners on two different substrates then get one conflict regime instead of two.
workflows/*.json -merge
